INSERT INTO "problem" ("number", "slug", "title", "status", "areas", "tags", "posed_by", "posed", "statement", "body", "history", "references", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES
(1, $seed$yang-mills-mass-gap$seed$, $seed$Yang–Mills existence and mass gap$seed$, 'open', ARRAY[$seed$quantum-field-theory$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Gauge theory$seed$, $seed$Constructive QFT$seed$, $seed$Confinement$seed$, $seed$Millennium Prize Problems$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Arthur Jaffe$seed$, $seed$Edward Witten$seed$]::text[], $seed$2000-05-24$seed$, $seed$Prove that for any compact simple gauge group $G$, a non-trivial quantum Yang–Mills theory exists on $\mathbb{R}^4$ and satisfies axioms at least as strong as the Wightman (or Osterwalder–Schrader) axioms, and that it has a mass gap $\Delta > 0$: every state orthogonal to the vacuum has energy at least $\Delta$.$seed$, $seed$## Background

Classical Yang–Mills theory with gauge group $G$ is described by a connection $A$ with curvature $F = \mathrm{d}A + A \wedge A$ and action

$$
S[A] = \frac{1}{4g^2} \int_{\mathbb{R}^4} \operatorname{tr}\, F_{\mu\nu} F^{\mu\nu} \, \mathrm{d}^4 x .
$$

Its quantization underlies the Standard Model: quantum chromodynamics is Yang–Mills theory with $G = SU(3)$ coupled to quarks. Perturbatively the theory is asymptotically free, so the coupling becomes weak at short distances. At long distances the coupling grows and perturbation theory breaks down, which is where confinement and the mass gap are expected to appear.

Lattice gauge theory gives strong numerical evidence for both. What is missing is a mathematical construction of the continuum theory in four dimensions, and a proof that its spectrum is gapped.

## Partial progress

Constructive field theory built interacting theories in two and three dimensions, such as $\phi^4_2$, $\phi^4_3$ and Yang–Mills in lower dimensions. Bałaban's renormalization-group analysis of lattice gauge theories established ultraviolet stability for four-dimensional Yang–Mills on a finite torus. Nobody has yet controlled the infinite-volume limit together with a proof of the gap.

## Significance

A solution would give the first rigorous example of an interacting four-dimensional quantum field theory. It would also give a mathematical explanation of why the strong force is short-ranged even though gluons are massless at the classical level.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"2000-05-24","kind":"posed","text":"Announced by the Clay Mathematics Institute as one of the seven Millennium Prize Problems."}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"A. Jaffe and E. Witten, Quantum Yang–Mills theory, Clay Mathematics Institute (2000)","url":"https://www.claymath.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/yangmills.pdf"},{"text":"K. G. Wilson, Confinement of quarks, Phys. Rev. D 10, 2445 (1974)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.10.2445"},{"text":"T. Bałaban, Renormalization group approach to lattice gauge field theories, Commun. Math. Phys. (1984–1989)"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-10-28$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-10-28$seed$::timestamptz),
(2, $seed$black-hole-information$seed$, $seed$The black-hole information problem$seed$, 'open', ARRAY[$seed$quantum-gravity$seed$, $seed$foundations$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Hawking radiation$seed$, $seed$Unitarity$seed$, $seed$Page curve$seed$, $seed$Holography$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Stephen Hawking$seed$]::text[], $seed$1976-11-15$seed$, $seed$Determine whether, and by what mechanism, the evaporation of a black hole formed from a pure quantum state $|\psi\rangle$ is unitary. In particular: derive the Page curve for the fine-grained entropy $S(\rho_{\mathrm{rad}})$ of the Hawking radiation from a microscopic theory of quantum gravity, and identify which assumption of Hawking's semiclassical argument fails.$seed$, $seed$## Background

Hawking showed that black holes radiate with an approximately thermal spectrum at temperature $T_H = \hbar c^3 / (8\pi G M k_B)$. If the radiation stays exactly thermal until the black hole has evaporated, a pure initial state ends as a mixed state, and that contradicts unitary quantum evolution.

Page observed that unitarity requires the entanglement entropy of the radiation to rise and then fall back to zero. The turnover, at the Page time, happens roughly when the black hole has lost half its initial Bekenstein–Hawking entropy $S_{BH} = A / 4G\hbar$.

## Partial progress

In AdS/CFT the boundary theory is manifestly unitary, which strongly suggests information is preserved. More recently, the quantum extremal surface prescription and replica wormholes reproduced the Page curve inside semiclassical gravity by including "island" contributions. It is still debated what these computations imply about the microscopic mechanism, how they relate to firewalls and the smoothness of the horizon, and how they extend to asymptotically flat spacetimes.

## Significance

A resolution would show which principles of quantum theory and general relativity survive in a complete theory of quantum gravity, whether unitarity, locality or the equivalence principle at the horizon. It is one of the sharpest consistency tests any candidate theory has to pass.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"1976-11-15","kind":"posed","text":"Hawking argues that black-hole evaporation turns pure states into mixed states."},{"date":"2019","kind":"advance","text":"Island and replica-wormhole computations reproduce the Page curve semiclassically."}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"S. W. Hawking, Breakdown of predictability in gravitational collapse, Phys. Rev. D 14, 2460 (1976)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.14.2460"},{"text":"D. N. Page, Information in black hole radiation, Phys. Rev. Lett. 71, 3743 (1993)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.71.3743"},{"text":"A. Almheiri, T. Hartman, J. Maldacena, E. Shaghoulian and A. Tajdini, The entropy of Hawking radiation, Rev. Mod. Phys. 93, 035002 (2021)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.93.035002"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-10-28$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-10-28$seed$::timestamptz),
(3, $seed$cosmological-constant$seed$, $seed$The cosmological constant problem$seed$, 'open', ARRAY[$seed$cosmology$seed$, $seed$quantum-field-theory$seed$, $seed$quantum-gravity$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Dark energy$seed$, $seed$Vacuum energy$seed$, $seed$Naturalness$seed$, $seed$Fine-tuning$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Yakov Zel'dovich$seed$, $seed$Steven Weinberg$seed$]::text[], $seed$1967$seed$, $seed$Explain why the observed vacuum energy density, $\rho_\Lambda \approx (2.3\ \mathrm{meV})^4$, is smaller by many orders of magnitude than the contributions expected from quantum field theory (of order $M^4$ for every massive field of mass $M$, and naively $M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^4$), without fine-tuning these contributions against a bare cosmological constant.$seed$, $seed$## Background

In general relativity the vacuum energy gravitates. A constant energy density $\rho_{\mathrm{vac}}$ enters Einstein's equations exactly like a cosmological constant,

$$
G_{\mu\nu} + \Lambda g_{\mu\nu} = 8\pi G\, T_{\mu\nu}, \qquad \Lambda_{\mathrm{eff}} = \Lambda + 8\pi G \rho_{\mathrm{vac}} .
$$

Zero-point fluctuations and phase transitions such as electroweak symmetry breaking and QCD condensation each contribute to $\rho_{\mathrm{vac}}$. These contributions exceed the observed value by roughly 55 orders of magnitude for the electroweak scale, and by up to 120 if one cuts off at the Planck scale.

## Partial progress

Proposed approaches include supersymmetry (which cancels the contributions only above the breaking scale), anthropic selection in a landscape of vacua, self-adjustment and sequestering mechanisms, modified gravity, and unimodular gravity. Weinberg's no-go theorem rules out a broad class of self-adjusting scalar-field solutions. None of the proposals is generally accepted.

## Significance

The problem is the starkest failure of naturalness in physics. A solution would probably change how quantum field theory couples to gravity, and it bears directly on the interpretation of dark energy.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"1967","kind":"posed","text":"Zel'dovich identifies the cosmological constant with the energy density of the quantum vacuum."},{"date":"1989","kind":"note","text":"Weinberg's review sets out the problem in its modern form."},{"date":"1998-09","kind":"advance","text":"Type Ia supernova observations show that the expansion of the universe is accelerating, consistent with a small positive $\\Lambda$."}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"Ya. B. Zel'dovich, Cosmological constant and elementary particles, JETP Lett. 6, 316 (1967)"},{"text":"S. Weinberg, The cosmological constant problem, Rev. Mod. Phys. 61, 1 (1989)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.61.1"},{"text":"A. G. Riess et al., Observational evidence from supernovae for an accelerating universe and a cosmological constant, Astron. J. 116, 1009 (1998)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/300499"},{"text":"J. Martin, Everything you always wanted to know about the cosmological constant problem (but were afraid to ask), C. R. Physique 13, 566 (2012)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crhy.2012.04.008"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-10-28$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-10-28$seed$::timestamptz),
(4, $seed$measurement-problem$seed$, $seed$The quantum measurement problem$seed$, 'open', ARRAY[$seed$foundations$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Decoherence$seed$, $seed$Interpretations$seed$, $seed$Born rule$seed$, $seed$Wave-function collapse$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$John von Neumann$seed$, $seed$Erwin Schrödinger$seed$]::text[], $seed$1932$seed$, $seed$Give a precise account of how definite measurement outcomes arise in quantum theory. Either show that unitary evolution $|\psi(t)\rangle = e^{-iHt/\hbar}|\psi(0)\rangle$ of system and apparatus alone yields single outcomes with Born-rule probabilities $p_k = |\langle k|\psi\rangle|^2$, or specify, and test, the additional dynamics or structure that does.$seed$, $seed$## Background

Quantum theory evolves states linearly. If an apparatus faithfully records the states $|0\rangle$ and $|1\rangle$ of a system, linearity forces a superposition $\alpha|0\rangle + \beta|1\rangle$ to end up entangled with the apparatus, $\alpha|0\rangle|A_0\rangle + \beta|1\rangle|A_1\rangle$, rather than in one of the two records. Textbook quantum mechanics adds a separate rule for measurement, but does not say what physically counts as one.

## Partial progress

Decoherence explains why interference between macroscopically distinct records becomes unobservable in practice, and why a preferred basis emerges. It does not by itself select a single outcome. The main programmes, Everettian quantum mechanics, Bohmian mechanics, objective-collapse models and epistemic interpretations, each answer the question differently. Collapse models make predictions that differ from standard quantum theory, and matter-wave interferometry and tests of spontaneous heating keep narrowing their parameters.

## Significance

The measurement problem decides what quantum theory says about the world, not only what it predicts. It also matters for quantum gravity and cosmology, where there is no external observer to perform measurements.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"1932","kind":"posed","text":"Von Neumann's axioms separate unitary evolution from the projection postulate for measurements."},{"date":"1957","kind":"advance","text":"Everett proposes the relative-state formulation, with no collapse."},{"date":"1986","kind":"advance","text":"Ghirardi, Rimini and Weber propose a spontaneous-collapse model with testable consequences."}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"J. von Neumann, Mathematische Grundlagen der Quantenmechanik, Springer (1932)"},{"text":"J. S. Bell, Against 'measurement', Physics World 3, 33 (1990)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/2058-7058/3/8/26"},{"text":"M. Schlosshauer, Decoherence, the measurement problem, and interpretations of quantum mechanics, Rev. Mod. Phys. 76, 1267 (2004)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.76.1267"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-10-20$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-10-20$seed$::timestamptz),
(5, $seed$strong-cp-problem$seed$, $seed$The strong CP problem$seed$, 'open', ARRAY[$seed$quantum-field-theory$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$QCD$seed$, $seed$Axion$seed$, $seed$Theta vacuum$seed$, $seed$Neutron electric dipole moment$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Gerard 't Hooft$seed$]::text[], $seed$1976$seed$, $seed$Explain why the CP-violating parameter of quantum chromodynamics, $\bar\theta = \theta - \arg\det M_q$, which multiplies the term $\frac{\bar\theta\, g_s^2}{32\pi^2} G^a_{\mu\nu}\tilde G^{a\,\mu\nu}$ in the Lagrangian, satisfies $|\bar\theta| \lesssim 10^{-10}$, although nothing in the Standard Model requires it to be small.$seed$, $seed$## Background

The QCD Lagrangian admits a term that violates P and CP. Because of instantons it cannot be removed by field redefinitions, and its coefficient combines the bare vacuum angle with the phase of the quark mass matrix. A value of order one would give the neutron an electric dipole moment about ten orders of magnitude larger than the experimental bound.

## Partial progress

The Peccei–Quinn mechanism promotes $\bar\theta$ to a dynamical field whose potential is minimized at zero. Its quantum, the axion, is also a dark-matter candidate and is searched for with haloscopes, helioscopes and astrophysical bounds. Alternatives include a massless up quark (disfavoured by lattice QCD) and models in which CP or P is an exact symmetry broken spontaneously (Nelson–Barr and left-right models). None has been confirmed experimentally.

## Significance

The strong CP problem is a sharp, quantitative fine-tuning puzzle inside the established Standard Model. A solution would point to new particles or symmetries, and an axion discovery would tie particle physics to the nature of dark matter.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"1976","kind":"posed","text":"'t Hooft shows that instantons make the QCD vacuum angle physical."},{"date":"1977","kind":"advance","text":"Peccei and Quinn propose a new chiral symmetry that relaxes $\\bar\\theta$ to zero dynamically."},{"date":"1978","kind":"advance","text":"Weinberg and Wilczek point out that the Peccei–Quinn mechanism predicts a light particle, the axion."},{"date":"2020","kind":"note","text":"The neutron EDM bound $|d_n| < 1.8 \\times 10^{-26}\\, e\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ tightens the limit on $\\bar\\theta$."}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"G. 't Hooft, Symmetry breaking through Bell–Jackiw anomalies, Phys. Rev. Lett. 37, 8 (1976)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.37.8"},{"text":"R. D. Peccei and H. R. Quinn, CP conservation in the presence of pseudoparticles, Phys. Rev. Lett. 38, 1440 (1977)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.38.1440"},{"text":"C. Abel et al., Measurement of the permanent electric dipole moment of the neutron, Phys. Rev. Lett. 124, 081803 (2020)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.124.081803"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-10-18$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-10-18$seed$::timestamptz),
(6, $seed$hierarchy-problem$seed$, $seed$The hierarchy problem$seed$, 'open', ARRAY[$seed$quantum-field-theory$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Higgs boson$seed$, $seed$Naturalness$seed$, $seed$Supersymmetry$seed$, $seed$Electroweak scale$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Leonard Susskind$seed$, $seed$Gerard 't Hooft$seed$]::text[], $seed$1979$seed$, $seed$Explain why the electroweak scale, set by the Higgs mass $m_H \approx 125\ \mathrm{GeV}$, is so much smaller than the Planck scale $M_{\mathrm{Pl}} \approx 1.2 \times 10^{19}\ \mathrm{GeV}$, given that quantum corrections $\delta m_H^2 \sim \Lambda^2/16\pi^2$ from physics at a high scale $\Lambda$ would naturally drive $m_H$ up to $\Lambda$.$seed$, $seed$## Background

Fermion and gauge-boson masses are protected by chiral and gauge symmetries: their quantum corrections are proportional to the masses themselves. The Higgs mass has no such protection. If the Standard Model is an effective theory valid up to a scale $\Lambda$, keeping $m_H$ at 125 GeV seems to require a cancellation between the bare mass and the corrections to many decimal places.

## Partial progress

Classic solutions add structure close to the TeV scale: supersymmetry, a composite Higgs, or extra dimensions that lower the fundamental scale of gravity. The LHC has not found the new particles these scenarios most naturally predict, which pushes them into more tuned regions. This has revived alternatives: cosmological relaxation of the Higgs mass, anthropic selection in a landscape of vacua, and the view that naturalness is the wrong criterion.

## Significance

The hierarchy problem has shaped experimental particle physics for four decades. Whether it is solved by new physics or dissolved by a better understanding of effective field theory will decide what to expect at future colliders.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"1979","kind":"posed","text":"Susskind points out that elementary scalars are unnatural and motivates technicolor."},{"date":"1980","kind":"advance","text":"'t Hooft formulates the naturalness criterion in terms of symmetries."},{"date":"2012-07-04","kind":"note","text":"ATLAS and CMS discover a Higgs boson with $m_H \\approx 125\\ \\mathrm{GeV}$."}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"L. Susskind, Dynamics of spontaneous symmetry breaking in the Weinberg–Salam theory, Phys. Rev. D 20, 2619 (1979)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.20.2619"},{"text":"G. 't Hooft, Naturalness, chiral symmetry, and spontaneous chiral symmetry breaking, NATO Sci. Ser. B 59, 135 (1980)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4684-7571-5_9"},{"text":"G. F. Giudice, Naturally speaking - the naturalness criterion and physics at the LHC (2008)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0801.2562"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-10-12$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-10-12$seed$::timestamptz),
(7, $seed$nature-of-dark-matter$seed$, $seed$The nature of dark matter$seed$, 'open', ARRAY[$seed$cosmology$seed$, $seed$quantum-field-theory$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$WIMPs$seed$, $seed$Axion$seed$, $seed$Galaxy rotation curves$seed$, $seed$CMB$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Fritz Zwicky$seed$, $seed$Vera Rubin$seed$]::text[], $seed$1933$seed$, $seed$Identify the constituent of the non-baryonic matter that makes up about $85\%$ of the matter in the universe, with present density $\Omega_c h^2 = 0.120 \pm 0.001$: determine its particle (or other) nature, mass and non-gravitational interactions, and explain how it was produced in the early universe.$seed$, $seed$## Background

Galaxy rotation curves, cluster dynamics, gravitational lensing, the acoustic peaks of the cosmic microwave background and the growth of large-scale structure all point to the same conclusion: most matter neither emits nor absorbs light and interacts at most weakly with ordinary matter. Its gravitational effects are measured precisely; its identity is unknown.

## Partial progress

Candidates span more than eighty orders of magnitude in mass: ultralight bosons, QCD axions, sterile neutrinos, weakly interacting massive particles (WIMPs) and primordial black holes. Direct-detection experiments have excluded much of the simplest WIMP parameter space, and axion haloscopes have begun to probe the QCD axion band. Modified gravity has difficulty explaining the CMB and the Bullet Cluster without some dark component.

## Significance

Dark matter is direct evidence for physics beyond the Standard Model. Identifying it would fix a central input of cosmology and would very likely reveal a new sector of particle physics.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"1933","kind":"posed","text":"Zwicky infers a large amount of unseen mass from galaxy velocities in the Coma cluster."},{"date":"1970","kind":"advance","text":"Rubin and Ford measure flat rotation curves out to large radii in the Andromeda galaxy."},{"date":"2018","kind":"advance","text":"Planck's final data fix the cold dark matter density to about one percent."}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"F. Zwicky, Die Rotverschiebung von extragalaktischen Nebeln, Helv. Phys. Acta 6, 110 (1933); English translation in Gen. Relativ. Gravit. 41, 207 (2009)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10714-008-0707-4"},{"text":"V. C. Rubin and W. K. Ford, Rotation of the Andromeda nebula from a spectroscopic survey of emission regions, Astrophys. J. 159, 379 (1970)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/150317"},{"text":"G. Bertone and D. Hooper, History of dark matter, Rev. Mod. Phys. 90, 045002 (2018)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.90.045002"},{"text":"Planck Collaboration, Planck 2018 results. VI. Cosmological parameters, Astron. Astrophys. 641, A6 (2020)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201833910"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-10-25$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-10-25$seed$::timestamptz),
(8, $seed$baryon-asymmetry$seed$, $seed$The matter–antimatter asymmetry$seed$, 'open', ARRAY[$seed$cosmology$seed$, $seed$quantum-field-theory$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Baryogenesis$seed$, $seed$Leptogenesis$seed$, $seed$Sakharov conditions$seed$, $seed$CP violation$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Andrei Sakharov$seed$]::text[], $seed$1967$seed$, $seed$Explain the origin of the baryon asymmetry of the universe, $\eta = (n_B - n_{\bar B})/n_\gamma \approx 6.1 \times 10^{-10}$, from a dynamical mechanism acting on a symmetric initial state, and identify the source of CP violation and the departure from thermal equilibrium that it requires.$seed$, $seed$## Background

The observable universe contains matter but almost no antimatter, and big-bang nucleosynthesis and the CMB independently measure the excess. Inflation would have diluted any initial asymmetry, so it must have been generated afterwards. Sakharov's conditions require baryon-number violation, C and CP violation, and a departure from thermal equilibrium.

## Partial progress

The Standard Model has all three ingredients in principle, but its CP violation is far too weak and, with a 125 GeV Higgs, the electroweak transition is a smooth crossover rather than a strong first-order transition. Viable mechanisms therefore need new physics: leptogenesis from heavy neutrinos (closely tied to neutrino masses), electroweak baryogenesis with an extended Higgs sector, Affleck–Dine baryogenesis in supersymmetric theories, and others. Electric dipole moment searches and neutrino experiments constrain several of them.

## Significance

Without an asymmetry there would be no atoms, stars or observers. Its origin is one of the few places where cosmology demands physics beyond the Standard Model and points to where it might be found.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"1967","kind":"posed","text":"Sakharov states three conditions any mechanism for generating a baryon asymmetry must satisfy."},{"date":"1985","kind":"advance","text":"Kuzmin, Rubakov and Shaposhnikov show that electroweak sphalerons violate baryon number efficiently in the early universe."},{"date":"1986","kind":"advance","text":"Fukugita and Yanagida propose leptogenesis from the decay of heavy right-handed neutrinos."}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"A. D. Sakharov, Violation of CP invariance, C asymmetry, and baryon asymmetry of the universe, JETP Lett. 5, 24 (1967); reprinted in Sov. Phys. Usp. 34, 392 (1991)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1070/PU1991v034n05ABEH002497"},{"text":"V. A. Kuzmin, V. A. Rubakov and M. E. Shaposhnikov, On anomalous electroweak baryon-number non-conservation in the early universe, Phys. Lett. B 155, 36 (1985)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(85)91028-7"},{"text":"M. Fukugita and T. Yanagida, Baryogenesis without grand unification, Phys. Lett. B 174, 45 (1986)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(86)91126-3"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-10-08$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-10-08$seed$::timestamptz),
(9, $seed$quantum-theory-of-gravity$seed$, $seed$A consistent quantum theory of gravity$seed$, 'open', ARRAY[$seed$quantum-gravity$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Renormalization$seed$, $seed$String theory$seed$, $seed$Asymptotic safety$seed$, $seed$Loop quantum gravity$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Gerard 't Hooft$seed$, $seed$Martinus Veltman$seed$]::text[], $seed$1974$seed$, $seed$Construct a quantum theory that is consistent at all energies (unitary, ultraviolet complete and predictive), reduces to general relativity coupled to the Standard Model below the Planck scale $E_{\mathrm{Pl}} = \sqrt{\hbar c^5/G} \approx 1.2 \times 10^{19}\ \mathrm{GeV}$, and makes at least one prediction that differs from semiclassical gravity and can in principle be tested.$seed$, $seed$## Background

Quantized as a field theory of the metric, general relativity works well as an effective theory at low energies. Its coupling $G$ has negative mass dimension, so perturbation theory produces new divergences at every loop order. Each needs a new counterterm, and the theory loses predictive power near the Planck scale.

## Partial progress

String theory gives a perturbatively finite, unitary framework that contains gravitons, and through holography it provides non-perturbative definitions in anti-de Sitter space. Asymptotic safety looks for a non-trivial ultraviolet fixed point using functional renormalization group methods. Loop quantum gravity, causal dynamical triangulations and causal sets pursue non-perturbative quantizations of geometry. Each programme still has open problems: a realistic de Sitter vacuum, rigorous control of truncations, or recovering smooth spacetime.

## Significance

A complete theory of quantum gravity would describe the big bang, the interiors of black holes and the microscopic structure of spacetime. It is needed to settle several other problems on this list, including the black-hole information problem.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"1974","kind":"posed","text":"'t Hooft and Veltman find non-renormalizable one-loop divergences in gravity coupled to matter."},{"date":"1979","kind":"advance","text":"Weinberg proposes asymptotic safety, an ultraviolet fixed point for gravity."},{"date":"1984","kind":"advance","text":"Green and Schwarz show anomaly cancellation in type I superstring theory."},{"date":"1986","kind":"note","text":"Goroff and Sagnotti find a two-loop divergence in pure gravity."}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"G. 't Hooft and M. Veltman, One-loop divergencies in the theory of gravitation, Ann. Inst. H. Poincaré A 20, 69 (1974)","url":"http://www.numdam.org/item/AIHPA_1974__20_1_69_0/"},{"text":"M. H. Goroff and A. Sagnotti, The ultraviolet behavior of Einstein gravity, Nucl. Phys. B 266, 709 (1986)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(86)90193-8"},{"text":"M. B. Green and J. H. Schwarz, Anomaly cancellations in supersymmetric D = 10 gauge theory and superstring theory, Phys. Lett. B 149, 117 (1984)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(84)91565-X"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-10-27$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-10-27$seed$::timestamptz),
(10, $seed$origin-of-neutrino-masses$seed$, $seed$The origin of neutrino masses$seed$, 'open', ARRAY[$seed$quantum-field-theory$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Neutrino oscillations$seed$, $seed$Seesaw mechanism$seed$, $seed$Majorana fermions$seed$, $seed$Neutrinoless double beta decay$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Bruno Pontecorvo$seed$]::text[], $seed$1998-06-05$seed$, $seed$Determine the mechanism that gives neutrinos their small masses $m_\nu \lesssim 0.45\ \mathrm{eV}$: establish whether neutrinos are Dirac or Majorana fermions, measure the absolute mass scale and the mass ordering, and explain why the masses are at least five orders of magnitude below those of the charged leptons.$seed$, $seed$## Background

In the Standard Model neutrinos are massless, because there are no right-handed neutrinos and lepton number is conserved. Oscillation experiments show that neutrinos change flavour, which requires at least two non-zero masses. The mass-squared differences are measured, but the absolute scale, the ordering and the nature of the mass are not.

## Partial progress

The seesaw mechanism explains small masses through heavy right-handed neutrinos, giving $m_\nu \sim m_D^2/M$ and Majorana neutrinos. It also connects to leptogenesis (Problem 8). Radiative and low-scale models are alternatives. Neutrinoless double beta decay would prove the Majorana nature; current searches with germanium and xenon set limits on its half-life above $10^{26}$ years. Cosmology bounds the sum of the masses, and oscillation experiments such as JUNO, DUNE and Hyper-Kamiokande target the ordering.

## Significance

Neutrino masses are the only laboratory evidence so far for physics beyond the Standard Model. Their origin may reveal a new mass scale and the violation of lepton number.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"1957","kind":"note","text":"Pontecorvo suggests that neutrinos could oscillate."},{"date":"1998-06-05","kind":"posed","text":"Super-Kamiokande reports atmospheric neutrino oscillations, so neutrinos must have mass."},{"date":"2022","kind":"advance","text":"KATRIN sets a direct upper limit below 1 eV on the effective electron-neutrino mass."}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"Super-Kamiokande Collaboration, Evidence for oscillation of atmospheric neutrinos, Phys. Rev. Lett. 81, 1562 (1998)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.81.1562"},{"text":"P. Minkowski, μ → eγ at a rate of one out of 10⁹ muon decays?, Phys. Lett. B 67, 421 (1977)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(77)90435-X"},{"text":"KATRIN Collaboration, Direct neutrino-mass measurement with sub-electronvolt sensitivity, Nature Phys. 18, 160 (2022)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-021-01463-1"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-09-30$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-09-30$seed$::timestamptz),
(11, $seed$low-entropy-past$seed$, $seed$The low-entropy initial state of the universe$seed$, 'open', ARRAY[$seed$foundations$seed$, $seed$cosmology$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Arrow of time$seed$, $seed$Past hypothesis$seed$, $seed$Second law$seed$, $seed$Weyl curvature$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Ludwig Boltzmann$seed$, $seed$Roger Penrose$seed$]::text[], $seed$1896$seed$, $seed$Explain why the early universe was in a state of extraordinarily low entropy, smooth and nearly homogeneous with gravitational degrees of freedom unexcited, so that the second law $\mathrm{d}S/\mathrm{d}t \ge 0$ holds in the direction we call the future. Either derive this initial condition from dynamics or a law of initial conditions, or show why no such explanation is needed.$seed$, $seed$## Background

The fundamental dynamical laws are, to excellent approximation, time-reversal invariant, yet macroscopic processes run in one direction. Statistical mechanics explains entropy increase towards the future only if the past is assumed to have lower entropy: the "past hypothesis". Gravity makes this sharper, because for gravitating systems a smooth configuration is a low-entropy one.

## Partial progress

Inflation explains some features of the early universe but presupposes a low-entropy patch to start from. Proposals include Penrose's Weyl curvature hypothesis and conformal cyclic cosmology, the Hartle–Hawking no-boundary state, bouncing cosmologies, and scenarios in which arrows of time point away from a minimal-entropy surface in both directions. None is generally accepted, and it is disputed whether the question calls for a dynamical explanation at all.

## Significance

The arrow of time underlies memory, causation and the very notion of records and measurements. Its explanation connects statistical mechanics, cosmology and quantum gravity.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"1896","kind":"posed","text":"Boltzmann, replying to Zermelo, attributes the second law to an improbable initial state of the universe."},{"date":"1979","kind":"advance","text":"Penrose proposes the Weyl curvature hypothesis and estimates the improbability of the initial state."}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"L. Boltzmann, Entgegnung auf die wärmetheoretischen Betrachtungen des Hrn. E. Zermelo, Ann. Phys. 293, 773 (1896)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/andp.18962930414"},{"text":"R. Penrose, Singularities and time-asymmetry, in General Relativity - An Einstein Centenary Survey, Cambridge University Press (1979)"},{"text":"S. M. Carroll and J. Chen, Spontaneous inflation and the origin of the arrow of time (2004)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0410270"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-09-14$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-09-14$seed$::timestamptz),
(12, $seed$hubble-tension$seed$, $seed$The Hubble tension$seed$, 'open', ARRAY[$seed$cosmology$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Hubble constant$seed$, $seed$Distance ladder$seed$, $seed$ΛCDM$seed$, $seed$CMB$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Planck Collaboration$seed$, $seed$SH0ES team$seed$]::text[], $seed$2013$seed$, $seed$Resolve the discrepancy between the Hubble constant inferred from the cosmic microwave background within $\Lambda$CDM, $H_0 = 67.4 \pm 0.5\ \mathrm{km\,s^{-1}\,Mpc^{-1}}$, and the value from the local Cepheid–supernova distance ladder, $H_0 \approx 73\ \mathrm{km\,s^{-1}\,Mpc^{-1}}$: determine whether it comes from unrecognized systematic errors or from physics beyond $\Lambda$CDM, and in the latter case which.$seed$, $seed$## Background

The Hubble constant can be measured directly, by calibrating distances to nearby galaxies and type Ia supernovae, or inferred indirectly, by fitting the standard cosmological model to the CMB and baryon acoustic oscillations. As both kinds of measurement became more precise, they settled on values that disagree well beyond their quoted uncertainties.

## Partial progress

On the observational side, independent calibrations (tip of the red giant branch, J-region asymptotic giant branch stars, masers, lensing time delays) are used to test the Cepheid ladder; some give values between the two camps. On the theoretical side, proposals that change the expansion history before recombination, such as early dark energy or extra relativistic species, can raise the CMB value but tend to worsen fits to other data. No model has gained broad support.

## Significance

If the tension is physical, it is the first crack in $\Lambda$CDM from precision cosmology and points to new ingredients in the early universe. If not, finding the systematic error will sharpen every distance measurement in astronomy.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"2013","kind":"posed","text":"The first Planck cosmological results give a lower $H_0$ than local distance-ladder measurements."},{"date":"2022","kind":"note","text":"SH0ES reports $H_0 = 73.0 \\pm 1.0$, about five standard deviations from the Planck value."},{"date":"2024","kind":"note","text":"JWST distance measurements are used to cross-check the Cepheid calibration; analyses with different distance indicators do not yet agree."}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"Planck Collaboration, Planck 2018 results. VI. Cosmological parameters, Astron. Astrophys. 641, A6 (2020)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201833910"},{"text":"A. G. Riess et al., A comprehensive measurement of the local value of the Hubble constant with 1 km/s/Mpc uncertainty from the Hubble Space Telescope and the SH0ES team, Astrophys. J. Lett. 934, L7 (2022)","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2041-8213/ac5c5b"},{"text":"E. Di Valentino et al., In the realm of the Hubble tension - a review of solutions, Class. Quantum Grav. 38, 153001 (2021)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/1361-6382/ac086d"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-10-26$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-10-26$seed$::timestamptz),
(13, $seed$solar-neutrino-problem$seed$, $seed$The solar neutrino problem$seed$, 'solved', ARRAY[$seed$quantum-field-theory$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Neutrino oscillations$seed$, $seed$Standard solar model$seed$, $seed$MSW effect$seed$, $seed$SNO$seed$]::text[], ARRAY[$seed$Raymond Davis Jr.$seed$, $seed$John Bahcall$seed$]::text[], $seed$1968$seed$, $seed$Explain why radiochemical and water-Cherenkov detectors observe only about one third to one half of the electron neutrinos predicted by the standard solar model, e.g. a rate of about $2.6\ \mathrm{SNU}$ in the chlorine experiment against a prediction near $8\ \mathrm{SNU}$, and determine whether the deficit lies in solar physics or in the properties of neutrinos.$seed$, $seed$## Background

Nuclear fusion in the Sun's core produces a calculable flux of electron neutrinos. For three decades every experiment, with different thresholds and techniques, saw too few of them. Either the solar models were wrong, or neutrinos did something on the way to Earth.

## Resolution

Helioseismology confirmed the solar models to high precision, making an astrophysical explanation increasingly unlikely. In 2002 the Sudbury Neutrino Observatory measured, with heavy water, both the electron-neutrino flux and the total flux of all three flavours. The total agreed with the standard solar model, while only about a third arrived as electron neutrinos. KamLAND's reactor experiment then confirmed the large-mixing-angle MSW solution. The deficit is explained by neutrino oscillations enhanced by matter in the Sun.

## Significance

The solution established that neutrinos have mass, the first confirmed departure from the minimal Standard Model (see Problem 10), and earned the 2002 and 2015 Nobel Prizes in Physics. It is an example of a long-standing anomaly that was taken seriously and turned out to be new physics.$seed$, $seed$[{"date":"1968","kind":"posed","text":"The Homestake chlorine experiment finds fewer solar neutrinos than predicted."},{"date":"1985","kind":"advance","text":"Mikheyev and Smirnov, building on Wolfenstein, show that matter can enhance flavour conversion inside the Sun."},{"date":"2002","kind":"status","text":"SNO's neutral-current measurement shows the total flux of all flavours agrees with the solar model; the missing electron neutrinos have changed flavour.","status":"solved"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$[{"text":"R. Davis, D. S. Harmer and K. C. Hoffman, Search for neutrinos from the Sun, Phys. Rev. Lett. 20, 1205 (1968)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.20.1205"},{"text":"SNO Collaboration, Direct evidence for neutrino flavor transformation from neutral-current interactions in the Sudbury Neutrino Observatory, Phys. Rev. Lett. 89, 011301 (2002)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.89.011301"},{"text":"J. N. Bahcall, M. H. Pinsonneault and S. Basu, Solar models - current epoch and time dependences, neutrinos, and helioseismological properties, Astrophys. J. 555, 990 (2001)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/321493"}]$seed$::jsonb, $seed$2026-08-02$seed$::timestamptz, $seed$2026-08-02$seed$::timestamptz);
